  {
   "title": "Japan’s International Telecommunications",
   "doc_type": "consular report",
   "start_page": 91,
   "end_page": 130,
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1939-02-14",
   "from": "W. Garland Richardson, American Vice Consul, Tokyo",
   "to": "Department of State",
   "about": "Surveys Japan’s international cables, wireless telegraphy, telephone services and overseas short-wave broadcasts.",
   "summary": "Richardson traces international cable and wireless development, describes the International Telecommunications Company, and reports proposed expansion of circuits and capital. Tables give telegraph and telephone rates, traffic totals and foreign connections, with a June 1938 wireless-network map. The report then details overseas broadcast schedules and analyzes January–March 1939 programs by destination and subject. It concludes that roughly two-thirds of broadcast time excluding announcements served news or political propaganda, while private short-wave reception in Japan was effectively prohibited.",
